I've spent the last 4 years reviewing API designs at work, in open-source PRs, and for consulting clients. Some patterns keep appearing — not because developers are bad, but because nobody teaches API design as a discipline.
Here are the 9 mistakes I see most often, with the exact fix for each one.
// GET /users/123 — success
{ "id": 123, "name": "Jane", "email": "jane@example.com" }

// GET /users/999 — not found  
{ "error": "User not found" }

// POST /users — success
{ "message": "User created", "userId": 456 }

// POST /users — validation error
{ "errors": ["Email is required", "Name too short"] }

Four endpoints, four different response shapes. Every consumer has to write custom parsing logic for each one.
The fix: One envelope, every response.
